==English==

===Etymology===
Share (2005) suggests that the adjective comes from the past {{glossary|participle}} of the verb {{m|en|airlock||to stop suddenly}},<ref>{{cite-book|year=2005|author=Share, Bernard|title=Slanguage: A Dictionary of Slang and Colloquial English in Ireland|page=3|text='''airlock''' [vb.] (Ulster). Stop in one’s tracks. Hence '''airlocked''' [p. part. as adj.]. The worse for drink.|location=Dublin|publisher=Gill & Macmillan}}</ref> which Macaffee (1996) says is “from the cut-out in a diesel engine if air enters the fuel system”.<ref>{{cite-book|year=1996|author=Macafee, Carolyn I.|title=A Concise Ulster Dictionary|location=Oxford|publisher=Oxford University Press}}</ref>

